// TEST: Two modules declaring one struct name (rejects)
// MODULE: Main
// ERROR: BL3001
// ERROR-CONTAINS: the parameter is declared A.P but the argument is B.P
// ============================================================================
// A struct's identity used to be its bare name, so A's `P` and B's `P` were
// ONE type to the checker -- `A.fx(B.mk())` passed A's field read a B value --
// and two global `struct P` definitions to g++; the program was refused
// (BL2009) as soon as two modules declared one type name. Identity is now the
// declaring module's (D11): both modules link (multifile/029), and handing
// B's `P` to A's function is the type mismatch it is, spelled qualified
// because the two source names coincide.
module Main
import A
import B
let q = B.mk()
let v = A.fx(q)
